  "summary": "San Jos\u00e9 officials plan to tighten rules on accessing the city's Flock ALPR database and petition the California Legislature to create criminal penalties for police misuse, after an SJPD officer was fired for using the system to help a relative track a domestic violence victim. The Santa Clara County DA said current law does not criminalize improper access by authorized officers. City leaders said they will strengthen audits but will not end the Flock contract.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "There is no law that makes it a crime for a police officer to improperly access an agency's license plate reader database. Only the state legislature can make this conduct a crime.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "an off-duty officer used a personal device to perform unauthorized license plate reader searches and locate a victim's vehicle. He then shared the location with the suspect, who was a relative of the officer.",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "the city does not plan to change or eliminate its contract with Flock Safety, citing public safety.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
  ],
